  <!-- 1. About the Work -->
  <section id="about">
    <div class="wrap col">
      <h2>About the Work</h2>
      <p>I help professionals and teams working across East Africa make better sense of communication, behaviour and relationships when the usual assumptions do not quite fit.</p>
      <p>My work brings together practical Swahili, cross-cultural communication and East Africa readiness.</p>
      <p>The common thread is interpretation: helping people slow down, examine what is happening, and respond with greater understanding rather than relying too quickly on assumptions about people, language or culture.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. What I Do -->
  <section id="what-i-do">
    <div class="wrap col">
      <h2>What I Do</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="do-grid">
        <div class="do-item">
          <h3>Practical Swahili</h3>
          <p>I teach Swahili for communication people can actually use.</p>
          <p>Lessons are built around the learner's starting point, situation and communication needs rather than treating language as a collection of vocabulary and grammar to be memorised.</p>
          <p>Depending on the learner and context, this can include:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>introductions and self-introductions</li>
            <li>greetings and everyday interaction</li>
            <li>asking and answering basic questions</li>
            <li>making requests</li>
            <li>asking someone to slow down or explain</li>
            <li>communicating about work and daily activities</li>
            <li>basic communication in community settings</li>
            <li>listening and responding in practical situations</li>
          </ul>
          <p>I also pay attention to what language is doing socially. A phrase may carry meanings related to respect, relationship, familiarity, age, role or situation that are not obvious from a literal translation.</p>
          <p>My approach therefore combines language foundations, practical scenarios, listening and response practice, and attention to the social meaning of language.</p>
          <p>The goal is usable communication, not simply accumulating vocabulary or grammar knowledge.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="do-item">
          <h3>Scenario-based learning</h3>
          <p>Where appropriate, I use real or realistic communication situations within lessons so that learners practise language in context.</p>
          <p>Rather than learning a phrase in isolation, the learner works with the situation in which that phrase may actually be needed: what is happening, what needs to be communicated, what language is appropriate, and how the interaction may continue.</p>
          <p>This allows language practice to remain connected to communication rather than becoming an exercise in memorising isolated expressions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="do-item">
          <h3>Cross-cultural communication</h3>
          <p>I help people examine confusing interactions before deciding what those interactions mean.</p>
          <p>When communication feels difficult across cultural difference, I encourage questions such as:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>What actually happened?</li>
            <li>What was said, and what did I interpret?</li>
            <li>Have I seen this more than once?</li>
            <li>Is this a pattern or a single interaction?</li>
            <li>What role, relationship or power dynamic is involved?</li>
            <li>Could an organisational or situational factor explain what happened?</li>
            <li>What other explanations are possible?</li>
            <li>What evidence supports a cultural explanation?</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The aim is to interpret carefully and respond appropriately when familiar assumptions no longer work.</p>
          <p>This is particularly relevant to people working across cultural difference within East African workplaces, organisations and communities.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="do-item">
          <h3>East Africa readiness</h3>
          <p>I am developing advisory work for professionals and teams preparing to work across East African contexts, as well as people already working in the region who encounter communication or relationship difficulties they did not anticipate.</p>
          <p>This work can include areas such as:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>communication expectations</li>
            <li>relationship-building</li>
            <li>language and social meaning</li>
            <li>forms of address and social positioning</li>
            <li>workplace relationships and hierarchy</li>
            <li>unfamiliar communication patterns</li>
            <li>assumptions and expectations</li>
            <li>situations where communication does not unfold as expected</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The emphasis is practical preparation.</p>
          <p>The aim is not to give someone a fixed list of "how East Africans behave", but to help them become better prepared to observe, interpret and respond once they are actually working and communicating in the region.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. How I Approach Communication Across Difference -->
  <section id="approach">
    <div class="wrap col">
      <h2>How I Approach Communication Across Difference</h2>
      <p>When a communication problem arises, I do not start by asking:</p>
      <p class="not-question">"What does this culture do?"</p>
      <p>I start with what actually happened.</p>
      <p>What was said? What was observed? Has this happened before? Is it a pattern or a single interaction? What role, relationship, power dynamic or organisational factor might be involved? What other explanations are possible?</p>
      <p>Only then do I ask whether cultural context helps explain what is happening, and how much weight it should carry.</p>
      <p>This matters because cultural explanations can sometimes help us understand an interaction. They can also become shortcuts that prevent us from seeing the individual, the relationship, the organisation or the immediate situation.</p>
      <p>My work is therefore concerned with:</p>

      <div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="Observation leads to Context, which leads to Interpretation, which leads to Response">
        <span>Observation</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>Context</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>Interpretation</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>Response</span>
      </div>

      <p>rather than jumping directly from an unfamiliar behaviour to a cultural conclusion.</p>
      <p>This approach informs both my teaching and my developing East Africa readiness work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

